  <section id="what-it-is">
    <h2>What this article is</h2>
    <p>
      This is a <strong>Turkish translation of the Mirzoev article</strong>
      that is already on this site, in two English versions:
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/circassian-school-in-syria/">the first translation</a>
      and the
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/circassian-school-in-syria-full-translation/">full translation with notes</a>.
      Jineps credits it to aheku.net, the site the first version came from,
      and not to the journal. So it is <strong>not an independent source</strong>
      for any of its facts. It is a Russian text rendered into Turkish, and
      that is the only reason this page exists.
    </p>
    <p>
      The article is someone else’s published work, and the translator’s, so I
      have <strong>not</strong> translated it in full. This page summarizes it
      in my own words, translates a few short sentences from the Turkish, and
      lists where the Turkish differs from the Russian-based versions on this
      site. Please have a Turkish reader check my translations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of
      or that disagrees with another source.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="summary">
    <h2>Summary</h2>
    <p>
      The article follows Mirzoev’s order, with section headings that are
      probably Jineps’s own. Three pull quotes from the text sit at the top.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Background.</strong> Circassian cultural “enlightenment”
        activity abroad began in the second half of the 19th century, after the
        Russian-Caucasian War. It grew out of a wish to keep a national identity
        in a foreign-language setting, out of the rise of the diaspora’s own
        educated class, and out of the absence of a state of their own. In Syria
        it was led by a group headed by Samgug (“Samkough-Semkough”) Emin,
        1900–1953.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>His life.</strong> Born in Syria to an Abzeh (Abadzekh) family;
        primary school in Quneitra, secondary school in Damascus, studies in
        Beirut in the 1920s, then the Sorbonne. He gathered material on
        Circassian history in France and wrote books that were published in
        1984. He was mayor of Quneitra from 1928 to 1936 and was elected to the
        Syrian parliament in 1931.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The society (1927).</strong> A group of like-minded Circassians
        founded a society to promote education and culture. Its 1928 charter, in
        Ottoman Turkish, was translated into Russian by B. N. Berezgov in 2017.
        It aimed to protect the mother tongue and customs, encourage national
        literature, and ask the local and French colonial authorities for
        autonomy for the Quneitra Circassians. It had a nine-person central
        committee, local branches and an annual meeting on 10 May, which was
        declared a national holiday.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Newspaper and publishing.</strong> A Latin-alphabet Circassian
        primer in 1929 (edited by Balagh Naubatuk), and the newspaper
        <em>Marje</em> from 7 October 1927 to 1932, in four languages. It was
        sent to Circassian communities in Jordan, Egypt and Turkey. A Circassian
        Literary Union and a sports club were also created. The society ended in
        1932 “for political reasons”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The school (1933).</strong> Opened in Quneitra with French
        support, paid for by public funds, private donations, shops opened by
        Samkough and other supporters, and part of the pay of Circassian
        cavalry soldiers in the French forces. The article lists the teachers,
        among them two women, and several who were poets. The first director
        died in April 1933, and Akif Hunagu took over. Grades 1–3 were taught
        only in Circassian, Arabic was added in grades 4–5 and French from grade
        5. Pupils sang a Circassian anthem written by Samkough each morning.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Festivals and sport.</strong> National dress, an orchestra,
        dances, poetry contests, a school theatre, sports days, and two football
        teams among the Golan Circassians.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Closure (1943).</strong> The article blames the Second World War
        and the rise of a pro-Arab faction among the Circassians, backed by
        landowning families, quoting A. Kushkhabiev on their opposition to
        Circassian autonomy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>1936–1937.</strong> After an armed clash on 9 September 1936
        between Samkough’s supporters and the pro-Arab “Iron Shirt” group, the
        society was banned and Samkough lost the mayoralty. In 1937 he and
        others formed “Jolan” (Golan) to promote a mass return to the Caucasus.
        A request to the Soviet embassy in Paris was refused, Jolan was
        suspended, and Samkough kept to education and scholarship until the
        school closed in 1943.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
