  <section id="review">
    <h2>Things to review</h2>

    <h3>Differences from the Russian-based versions on this site</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>studies.</strong> The Turkish says he received
          <span lang="tr">«ziraat mühendisliği eğitimi»</span> (“agricultural
          engineering education”) in Beirut. The full translation of the
          Russian on this site says he “qualified as an agronomist”. These may
          not mean the same thing, and I can’t tell from here which word the
          Russian used.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the society’s name.</strong> The Turkish gives
          <span lang="tr">«Çerkes Eğitim ve Birlik Derneği»</span> (“Circassian
          Education and Unity Society”) as a literal translation “from the
          Circassian language”. The Russian-based version says “Upbringing and
          Unity”. Neither is the Circassian original, and the article later
          shortens the name again.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the name Samkough Emin.</strong> “Emin” is the
          Turkish form of Amin, and “Samkough” is the spelling this site uses.
          The Russian original has «Самгуг». The Turkish text adds the variants
          “Samkough-Semkough” once, in brackets. The translator seems to have
          chosen these spellings, so it doesn’t confirm how he spelled his own
          name.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>names in Russian spelling.</strong> Many other names
          keep Russian-style transliterations (for example “Xazhu”, “Jatao” and
          “Udzhikhu”). Some teachers’ names differ between versions, such as
          Rıfat, Rakhfat and Rufat Gutoko, and Yahya Çerkas versus Sherkas. The
          football teams’ names are given in Cyrillic with Turkish spellings
          (“Yamlic” and “Yamıblyaj”). All of these come through Russian, not
          from Circassian or Arabic originals.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>what was dropped.</strong> The Turkish leaves out the
          in-text references (such as Berezgov 2017, Kushkhabiev 1993 and the
          informants), the translator’s notes, and the sentence that the
          newspaper “therefore had four pages”. The full translation
          here says the books were produced as <em>samizdat</em> (self-published
          outside official channels). The Turkish just says the school
          published its own books, so that nuance is lost.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>small slips.</strong> One phrase is duplicated
          (“Bu olay, Bu olay”), and one heading is unformatted. I take these to
          be typing slips at Jineps.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Claims that conflict with, or aren’t confirmed by, other sources</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>school opening year.</strong> This article says 1933
          and also that the first director died in April 1933. Other pages on
          this site report 1932 (see the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/kushkhabiev-circassian-community-in-syria/">Kushkhabiev</a>
          and
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/khotko-circassians-in-syria/">Khotko</a>
          summaries). If it opened in 1933, the first director served only a few
          months.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the languages of <em>Marje</em>.</strong> The article
          says Circassian, Turkish, Arabic and French. The front page of issue
          no. 71 that I transcribed is in Ottoman Turkish with an Arabic box.
          See the <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/marj-newspaper/">Marj page</a>.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the end of his mayoralty.</strong> The article says he
          was “removed” (<span lang="tr">«görevinden alındı»</span>). Other
          sources on this site disagree about whether he was dismissed or
          resigned, and about whether he was appointed or elected in the first
          place.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the “Iron Shirt” clash and the 1937 Paris approach.</strong>
          Both appear in this article with no references, and, in the
          Kushkhabiev account, the clash is tied to the death of his brother.
          They have not been confirmed independently.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>publication history.</strong> The article was first
          published in 2019 in a journal (according to the full translation
          page), then on aheku.net, then here in 2022. Treat the dates as the
          dates of each copy.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
